.TH CLUNK 5
.SH NAME
clunk, remove \- forget about a fid, remove a file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B size[4] Tclunk tag[2] fid[4]
.B size[4] Rclunk tag[2]
.fi
.PP
.nf
.B size[4] Tremove tag[2] fid[4]
.B size[4] Rremove tag[2]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Tclunk
tells the server that
.I fid
is no longer needed.
The file itself is untouched.
Once a fid is clunked, the same number may be bound again by a fresh
.B Twalk
or
.BR Tattach .
Clunking a fid the server never bound is not worth an error,
because the client wanted it gone either way, and every server in
the tree answers
.B Rclunk
regardless.
.PP
.B Tremove
asks the server to remove the file
.I fid
names, and clunks
.I fid
whether or not the removal succeeds.
A caller must treat the fid as gone even when
.B Rremove
carries an error, and must not retry the removal on the same fid.
It is correct to think of
.B Tremove
as a
.B Tclunk
with the side effect of removing the file, when permission allows.
.PP
.IR kfs (4),
.IR fatfs (4)
and
.IR memfs (4)
implement
.BR Tremove ,
as does the kernel's
.B /srv
registry, where it takes a posted name out rather than touching a
disk.
A server over a fixed, read-only tree refuses it with the same
error
.B Tlcreate
gets there.
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/vectra9/proto.odin
.br
.B kernel/vfs/chan.odin
.br
.B kernel/srv/srv.odin
